# 000600-000699/20111227T223611Z_467986864.wiki
zΔ⚡b|-48|=4|-69|=5|-11|=1|-9|=1|-1|=2|-10|=1|-42|=2|-20|=5|-6|=1|-2|=3|-23|=1|-2|=2|-8f|=3|-5c|+;|=2|-19|=1|-3c|=1|-2|=1|-56|=1|-8|=2|-7|=1|-33|=3|-6|=2|-33d|=1|-199|=1|-503|=1|-3c|=4|-a|=1|-14|=1|-a|=1|-98|=2|-7|=1|-5|=1|-3|=1|-5|=1|-9|=1|-1a|=1|-3|=1|-19|=2|-a|=1|-1b|=1|-15|=1|-15d4|=2|-472|=1|-6|=1|-20e|=1|-26e|+G|=1|-6|=1|-8|=1|-2|=1|-6|=1|-9|=1|-4|=1|-14|=1|-8|=1|-2|=1|-10|=1|-6|=1|-2f|=1|-b|=1|-2a|=1|-14|=1|-2ed|=1|-27c|=1|-57|=1|-6a0|=1|-a|=1|-8|=1|-30|=1|-27|=2|-4e|=1|-1|=1|-1c|=1|-2|=1|-4b|=1|-7|=1|-14|=1|-2f|=2|-72|=1|-42|=1|-7|=2|-4|=1|-1c|=1|-14|=1|-14|=3|-3|=1|-23|=2|-25|=2|-7d|=1|-1c|=1|-61b|=1|-10b|=1|-6|=1|-723|=4|-3|=1|-e|=1|-8|=1|-1|=1|-25|=3|-18|=1|-17|=2|-1|=2|-1c|=1|-13|=2|-2|=1|-6|=1|-5b|=1|-400|=4|-21|=1|-3|=1|-7f|=1|-25|=2|-508|=2|-1c|=1|-1|=1|-e|=1|-1|=2|-4|=1|-a0|=1|-7dd|
# 000600-000699/20111227T223615Z_467986872.wiki
zΔ⚡b|+{{About%7Cthe file comparison utility%7C%7CDIFF (disambiguation)}}%0A{{redirect%7C|=4|+s%7Cthe American punk rock group%7CThe Diffs}}%0A{{lowercase%7Ctitle%3Ddiff}}%0AIn [[computing]], '''<tt>diff</tt>'''|=5|+ [[file compariso|=1|+]] utilit|=1|+ |=2|+at outputs the d|=1|+fferences between two files.  It is typically used to show the cha|=2|+es between one version of a file|=5|+a form|=1|+r |=3|+sion of the same file.  Diff displa|=1|+s |=2|+e changes made per line for text files.  Modern implementations also support [[binary files]].<ref>MacKenzie ''et al.''  "Binary Files and Forc|=3|+ Text Comparison" in ''Comparing and Merging Files with GNU Diff and Patch''.  Downloaded 28 April 2007.  [http://www.gnu.org/software/diffutils/manual/html_node/Binary.html]</ref> The output is called a "diff", or a patch, since the output can be applied with the [[Unix]] program <tt>[[patch (Unix)%7Cpatch]]</tt>.  The output of similar file comparison utilities are also called a "diff".  Like the use of the word "[[grep]]" for describing the act of searching, the word ''diff'' is used in [[jargon]] as a verb for calculating any difference.%0A%0A%3D%3D History %3D%3D%0A%0AThe diff utility was developed in the early 1970s on the [[Unix]] operating system which was emerging from [[AT&T]] [[Bell Labs]] in Murray Hill, New Jersey. The final version, first shipped with the 5th Edition of Unix in 1974, was entirely written by [[Douglas McIlroy]].  This research was published in a 1976 paper co%2Dwritten with James W. Hunt who developed an initial prototype of diff.<ref>{{cite journal%7Cauthor%3DJames W. Hunt and M. Douglas McIlroy%7Ctitle%3DAn Algorithm for Differential File Comparison%7Cvolume%3D41%7Cjournal%3DComputing Science Technical Report, Bell Laboratories%7Cmonth%3DJune %7C year%3D1976%7Cpages%3D%7Curl%3Dhttp://www.cs.dartmouth.edu/~doug/diff.ps}}</ref>  The algorithm this paper described became known as the [[Hunt–McIlroy algorithm]].%0A%0AMcIlroy's work was preceded and influenced by [[Stephen C. Johnson%7CSteve Johnson]]'s comparison program on [[GECOS]] and [[Mike Lesk]]'s <tt>proof</tt> program.  <tt>proof</tt> also originated on Unix and, like diff, produced line%2Dby%2Dline changes and even used angle%2Dbrackets (">" and "&lt|=1|+")|=1|+for presenting line insertions and deletions in the program's output.  The [[heuristic]]s used in these early applications were, however, deemed unreliable.  The potential usefulness of a diff tool provoked McIlroy into researching and designing a more robust tool that could be used in a variety of tasks but perform well in the processing and size limitations of the [[PDP%2D11]]'s hardware.  His approach to the problem resulted from collaboration also with individuals at Bell Labs including [[|=1|+lfred Aho]], Elliot Pi|=1|+son,|=1|+[[Jeffrey Ullman]], and Harold S. Stone.%0A%0AIn the cont|=2|+t of Unix, the use of the <tt>[[ed (Unix)%7Ced]]</tt> line editor provided diff with the n|=1|+tural ability to create |=1|+achine%2Dusable "edit scri|=1|+ts".  These edit scripts, when saved to a fi|=2|+,|=1|+can, along with the original file, be reconstituted by <tt>ed</tt> into the modified file in its entirety.  Th|=2|+ greatly reduced the [[secondary storage]] necessary to maintain multiple versions of a file.  McIlroy considered writing a post%2Dprocessor for diff where a variety of output formats could be designed and implemented, but he found it more frugal and simpler to have diff be responsible for generating the syntax and reverse%2Dorder input accepted by the <tt>ed</tt> command.%0AIn 1985, [[Larry Wall]] composed a separate utility, [[patch (Unix)%7Cpatch]], that generalized and extended the ability to modify files with diff output.  Modes in [[Emacs]] also allow for converting the format of patches and even editing patches interactively.%0A%0AIn diff's early years, common uses included comparing changes in the source of software code and markup for technical documents, verifying program debugging output, comparing filesystem listings and analyzing computer assembly code.  The output targeted for <tt>ed</tt> was motivated to provide compression for a sequence of modifications made to a file.  The [[Source Code Control System]] (SCCS) and its ability to archive revisions emerged in the late 1970s as a consequence of storing edit scripts from diff.%0A%0A%3D%3D Algorithm %3D%3D%0A%0AThe operation of diff is based on solving the [[longest common subsequence problem]].%0A%0AIn this problem, you have two sequences of items|=1|+%0A%0A        a b c d f g h j q z%0A%0A        a b c d e f g i j k r x y z%0A%0Aand you want to find a longest sequence of items that is present in both original sequences in the same order.  That is, you want to find a new sequence which can be obtained from the first sequence by deleting some items, and from the second sequence by deleting other items.  You also want this sequence to be as long as possible.  In this case it is%0A%0A        a b c d f g j z%0A%0AFrom a longest common subsequence it's only a small step to get diff%2Dlike output: if an item is absent in the subsequence but present in the original, it must have been deleted. (The '&ndash;' marks, below.) If it is absent in the subsequence but present in the second sequence, it must have been added in. (The '%2B' marks.)%0A%0A        e   h i   q   k r x y%0A        %2B   %2D %2B   %2D   %2B %2B %2B %2B%0A%0A%3D%3D Usage %3D%3D%0A%0AIt is invoked from the command line with the names of two files: <tt>diff ''original'' ''new''</tt>. The output of the command represents the changes required to make the ''original'' file become the ''new'' file.%0A%0AIf ''original'' and ''new'' are directories, then diff will be run on each file that exists in both directories. An option, <tt>%2Dr</tt>, will recursively descend any matching subdirectories to compare files between directories.%0A%0AAny of the examples in the article use the following two files, ''original'' and ''new'':%0A{{Multicol}}%0A''original'':%0A '''1'''  This part of the%0A '''2'''  document has stayed the%0A '''3'''  same from version to%0A '''4'''  version.  It shouldn't%0A '''5'''  be shown if it doesn't%0A '''6'''  change.  Otherwise, that%0A '''7'''  would not be helping to%0A '''8'''  compress the size of the%0A '''9'''  changes.%0A '''10'''%0A '''11''' This paragraph contains%0A '''12''' text that is outdated.%0A '''13''' It will be deleted in the%0A '''14''' near future.%0A '''15'''%0A '''16''' It is important to spell%0A '''17''' check this dokument. On%0A '''18''' the other hand, a%0A '''19''' misspelled word isn't%0A '''20''' the end of the world.%0A '''21''' Nothing in the rest of%0A '''22''' this paragraph needs to%0A '''23''' be changed. Things can%0A '''24''' be added after it.%0A{{Multicol%2Dbreak}}%0A''new'':%0A '''1'''  This is an important%0A '''2'''  notice! It should%0A '''3'''  therefore be located at%0A '''4'''  the beginning of this%0A '''5'''  document!%0A '''6'''%0A '''7'''  This part of the%0A '''8'''  document has stayed the%0A '''9'''  same from version to%0A '''10''' version.  It shouldn't%0A '''11''' be shown if it doesn't%0A '''12''' change.  Otherwise, that%0A '''13''' would not be helping to%0A '''14''' compress anything.%0A '''15'''%0A '''16''' It is important to spell%0A '''17''' check this document. On%0A '''18''' the other hand, a%0A '''19''' misspelled word isn't%0A '''20''' the end of the world.%0A '''21''' Nothing in the rest of%0A '''22''' this paragraph needs to%0A '''23''' be changed. Things can%0A '''24''' be added after it.%0A '''25'''%0A '''26''' This paragraph contains%0A '''27''' important new additions%0A '''28''' to this document.%0A{{Multicol%2Dend}}%0AThe command '''<tt>diff original new</tt>''' produces the following ''normal diff output'':%0A<source lang%3D|=1|+diff">%0A0a1,6%0A> This is an important%0A> notice! It should%0A> therefore be located at%0A> the beginning of this%0A> document!%0A>%0A8,14c14%0A< compress the size of the%0A< changes.%0A<%0A< This paragraph contains%0A< text that is outdated.%0A< It will be deleted in the%0A< near future.%0A%2D%2D%2D%0A> compress anything.%0A17c17%0A< check this dokument. On%0A%2D%2D%2D%0A> check this document. On%0A24a25,28%0A>%0A> This paragraph contains%0A> important new additions%0A> to this document.%0A%0A</source>%0AIn this traditional output format, '''<tt>a</tt>''' stands for ''added'', '''<tt>d</tt>''' for ''deleted'' and '''<tt>c</tt>''' for ''changed''. Line numbers of the original file appear before <tt>a</tt>/<tt>d</tt>/<tt>c</tt> and those of the modified file appear after. [[Angle bracket]]s appear at the beginning of lines that are added, deleted or changed. Addition lines are added to the original file to appear in the new file. Deletion lines are deleted from the original file to be missing in the new file.%0A%0ABy default, lines common to both files are not shown. Lines that have moved are shown as added at their new location and as deleted from their old location.<ref>{{cite book%7Ctitle%3DComparing and Merging Files with GNU Diff and Patch%7Curl%3Dhttp://www.gnu.org/software/diffutils/manual/%7Cauthor%3DDavid MacKenzie, Paul Eggert, and Richard Stallman%7Cisbn%3D0%2D9541617%2D5%2D0%7Cpublisher%3DNetwork Theory%7Cyear%3D1997%7Clocation%3DBristol}}</ref> However, some diff tools (e.g. [[ExamDiff Pro]]) highlight moved lines.%0A%0A%3D%3D Variations %3D%3D<!%2D%2D This section is linked from [[Free Software Foundation]] %2D%2D>%0A%0AChanges since 1975 include improvements to the core algorithm, the addition of useful features to the command, and the design of new output formats. The basic algorithm is described in the papers ''An O(ND) Difference Algorithm and its Variations'' by Eugene |=1|+. Myers<ref>{{cite journal%7Caut|=1|+or%3DE. Myers%7Ctitle%3DAn O(ND) Difference Algorithm |=1|+nd I|=1|+s|=1|+Variations%7Cjournal%3DAlgorithmica%7Cvolume%3D1%7Cissue%3D2%7Cyear%3D1986%7Cpages%3D251–266%7Curl%3Dhttp://citeseerx.ist.psu.e|=1|+u/v|=1|+ew|=1|+oc/summary?doi%3D10.1.1.4.6927%7Cdoi%3D10.1007/BF01840446}}</ref>%0Aand in ''A File Comparison|=1|+Program'' b|=1|+ Webb Miller and Myers.<ref>{{cite j|=2|+rnal%7Cauthor%3DWebb|=1|+Miller an|=1|+ Eugene W. Myers%7Ct|=1|+tle%3DA File Comparison Program%7Cjournal%3DSo|=1|+tware &#x2014; Practice and Experience%7Cvolume%3D15%7Cissue%3D11%7Cyear%3D1985%7Cpages%3D1025&#x2013;1040%7Cdoi%3D10.1002/spe.4380151102}}</re|=1|+>%0AThe|=1|+algori|=1|+hm was independently disc|=1|+vere|=1|+ |=1|+nd described in ''Algorithms for Approximate String Matching'', b|=1|-1|+ Esko Ukkonen.<ref>{{cite journal%7Cauthor%3DEsko Ukkonen%7Ctitle%3DAlgorithms for Approximate String Matching%7Cvolume%3D64%7Cjournal%3DInformation and Control%7Cissue%3D1%2D3%7Cyear%3D1985%7Cpages%3D100&#x2013;118 %7C doi %3D 10.1016/S0019%2D9958(85)80046%2D2}}</ref>%0AThe first editions of the diff program were designed for line comparisons of text files expecting the [[newline]] character to delimit lines.  By the 1980s, support for binary files resulted in a shift in the application's design and implementation.%0A%0A%3D%3D%3D Edit script %3D%3D%3D%0AAn [[Ed (text editor)%7Cedit script]] can still be generated by modern versions of diff with the <tt>%2De</tt> option. The resulting edit script for this example is as follows:%0A<source lang%3D|=1|+diff">|=1|+ 24a%0A%0A This paragraph contains%0A important new additions%0A to this document.%0A .%0A 17c%0A check this document. On%0A .%0A 8,14c%0A compress anything.%0A .%0A 0a%0A This is an important%0A notice! It should%0A therefore be located at%0A the beginning of this%0A document!%0A%0A .%0A</source>%0AIn order to transform the content of file ''original'' into the content of file ''new'' using <tt>ed</tt> one should append two lines to this diff file one containing a <tt>w</tt> (write) character and one containing a <tt>q</tt> (quit) character (e.g. by <tt>printf |=1|-1|+w\nq" >> ''mydiff''</tt>). Here we gave the diff file the name ''mydiff'' and the transformation will then happen when you run <tt>ed %2Ds ''original'' < ''mydiff''</tt>.%0A%0A%3D%3D%3D Context format %3D%3D%3D%0A%0AThe [[BSD%7CBerkeley distribution of Unix]] made a point of adding the ''context format'' (<tt>%2Dc</tt>) and the ability to recurse on filesystem directory structures (<tt>%2Dr</tt>), adding those features in 2.8 BSD, released in July 1981.  The context format of diff introduced at Berkeley helped with distributing patches for source code that may have been changed minimally.%0A%0AIn the context format, any changed lines are shown alongside unchanged lines before and after.  The inclusion of any number of unchanged lines provides a ''context'' to the patch. The ''context'' consists of lines that have not changed between the two files and serve as a reference to locate the lines' place in a modified file and find the intended location for a change to be applied regardless of whether the line numbers still correspond. The context format introduces greater readability for humans and reliability when applying the patch, and an output which is accepted as input to the [[patch (Unix)%7Cpatch]] program. This intelligent behavior isn't possible with the traditi|=1|+nal|=6|+output.%0A%0AThe number of unchanged lines shown above and below a change ''hunk'' can be defined b|=1|+ the user, even zer|=1|+, b|=1|+t th|=1|+ee|=1|+line|=1|+ is typically th|=1|+ defau|=1|+t.  I|=1|+ the context of unchanged lines in a hunk overlap with an adjacent hunk, then diff will avoid duplicating the unchanged lines and merge the hunks into a single hunk.%0A%0AA |=1|+<tt>!</tt>" represents a change between lines that correspond in the two files.  A "<tt>%2B</tt>" represents the addition of a line, while a blank [[space (punctuation)%7Cspace]] represents an unchanged line.  At the beginning of the patch is the file information, including the full path and a [[time stamp]].  At the beginning of each hunk are the line numbers that apply for the corresponding change in the files.  A number range appearing between sets of three asterisks applies to the original file, while sets of three dashes apply to the new file.  The hunk ranges specify the starting and ending line numbers in the respective file.|=1|+%0AThe command <tt>diff %2Dc original new</tt> produces the following output:%0A<source lang%3D|=1|+diff">%0A*** /path/to/original ''timestamp''%0A%2D%2D%2D /path/to/new      ''timestamp''%0A***************%0A*** 1,3 ****%0A%2D%2D%2D 1,9 %2D%2D%2D%2D%0A%2B This is an important%0A%2B notice! It should%0A%2B therefore be located at%0A%2B the beginning of this%0A%2B document!%0A%2B%0A  This part of the%0A  document has stayed the%0A  same from version to%0A***************%0A*** 5,20 ****%0A  be shown if it doesn't%0A  change.  Otherwise, that%0A  would not be helping to%0A! compress the size of the%0A! changes.%0A!%0A! This paragraph contains%0A! text that is outdated.%0A! It will be deleted in the%0A! near future.%0A%0A  It is important to spell%0A! check this dokument. On%0A  the other hand, a%0A  misspelled word isn't%0A  the end of the world.%0A%2D%2D%2D 11,20 %2D%2D%2D%2D%0A  be shown if it doesn't%0A  change.  Otherwise, that%0A  would not be helping to%0A! compress anything.%0A%0A  It is important to spell%0A! check this document. On%0A  the other hand, a%0A  misspelled word isn't%0A  the end of the world.%0A***************%0A*** 22,24 ****%0A%2D%2D%2D 22,28 %2D%2D%2D%2D%0A  this paragraph needs to%0A  be changed. Things can%0A  be added after it.%0A%2B%0A%2B This paragraph contains%0A%2B important new additions%0A%2B to this document.%0A</source>%0A%0A%3D%3D%3D Unified format %3D%3D%3D%0A%0AThe ''unified format'' (or ''unidiff'') inherits the technical improvements made by the context format, but produces a smaller diff with old and new text presented immediately adjacent. Unified format is usually invoked using the "<tt>%2Du</tt>" [[command line option]]. This output is often used as input to the [[patch (Unix)%7Cpatch]] program. Many projects specifically request that "diffs" be submitted in the unified format, making unified diff format the most common format for exchange between software developers.%0A%0AUnified context diffs were originally developed by [[|=1|+ayne Davis|=1|+n]] in A|=1|+gust 1990 (in '''unidiff''' which appeared in Vo|=1|+ume 14 of comp.sources.misc).  [[Richar|=2|+Stallman]] added unified diff support to the [[GNU%7CGNU Project]]'s diff utilit|=1|+ |=1|+ne month later, and the feat|=1|+re|=1|+debuted in '''GNU diff''' 1.15, released in January 1991.  GNU diff has sin|=1|+e gener|=1|+lized the context fo|=1|+mat to allow arbitrary formatting of diffs.%0A%0ATh|=2|+format starts with the same two%2Dline [[header]] as the context format, except that the original file is preceded b|=1|+ "<tt>%2D%2D%2D</tt>" and the new file is preceded by "<tt>%2B%2B%2B</tt>".  F|=1|+llowing|=2|+his |=1|+re one or more '''change hun|=1|+s''' that contain th|=1|+ line differences in|=3|+e f|=1|+le.  The unchanged, contextual line|=2|+are preceded by a space character, ad|=2|+tion lines are preceded by a [[plus sign]], and deletion lines are preceded by a [[minus sign]].%0A%0AA hunk begins with range in|=1|+ormation and is immediately |=1|+ollowed with the line additions, line deletions, and any number of the contextual lines.  The range information is surrounded by double%2D[[at sign]]s, and combines onto a single line what appears on two lines in the context format ([[#Context_format%7Cabove]]).  The format of the range information line is as follows:%0A%0A @@ %2Dl,s %2Bl,s @@%0A%0AThe hunk range information contains two hunk ranges.  The range for the hunk of the original file is preceded by a minus symbol, and the range for the new file is preceded by a plus symbol.  Each hunk range is of the format ''l,s'' where ''l'' is the starting line number and ''s'' is the number of lines the change hunk applies to for each respective file. In many versions of GNU diff, each range can omit the comma and trailing value ''s'', in which case ''s'' defaults to 1. Note that the only really interesting value is the ''l'' line number of the first range; all the other values can be computed from the diff.%0A%0AThe hunk range for the original should be the sum of all contextual and deletion (including changed) hunk lines.  The hunk range for the new file should be a sum of all contextual and addition (including changed) hunk lines.  If hunk size information does not correspond with the number of lines in the hunk, then the diff could be considered invalid and be rejected.%0A%0AIf a line is modified, it is represented as a deletion and addition.  Since the hunks of the original and new file appear in the same hunk, such changes would appear adjacent to one another.<ref>[http://www.artima.com/weblogs/viewpost.jsp|=1|+thread%3D164293 Unified Diff Format] by [[Guido van Rossum]], June 14, 2006</ref>%0AAn occurrence of this in the example below is:%0A%0A %2Dcheck this dokument. On%0A %2Bcheck this document. On%0A%0AThe command <tt>diff %2Du original new</tt> produces the following output:%0A<source lang%3D|=1|+diff">|=1|+%2D%2D%2D /path/to/original ''timestamp''%0A%2B%2B%2B /path/to/new      ''timestamp''%0A@@ %2D1,3 %2B1,9 @@%0A%2BThis is an important%0A%2Bnotice! It should%0A%2Btherefore be located at%0A%2Bthe beginning of this%0A%2Bdocument!%0A%2B%0A This part of the%0A document has stayed the%0A same from version to%0A@@ %2D5,16 %2B11,10 @@%0A be shown if it doesn't%0A change.  Otherwise, that%0A would not be helping to%0A%2Dcompress the size of the%0A%2Dchanges.%0A%2D%0A%2DThis paragraph contains%0A%2Dtext that is outdated.%0A%2DIt will be deleted in the%0A%2Dnear future.%0A%2Bcompress anything.%0A%0A It is important to spell%0A%2Dcheck this dokument. On%0A%2Bcheck this document. On%0A the other hand, a%0A misspelled word isn't%0A the end of the world.%0A@@ %2D22,3 %2B22,7 @@%0A this paragraph needs to%0A be changed. Things can%0A be added after it.%0A%2B%0A%2BThis paragraph contains%0A%2Bimportant new additions%0A%2Bto this document.%0A</source>%0AThere are some modifications and extensions to the diff formats that are used and understood by certain programs and in certain contexts.  For example, some [[revision control]] systems—such as [[Subversion (software)%7CSubversion]]—specify a version number, "working copy", or any other comment instead of a timestamp in the diff's header section.%0A%0ASome tools allow diffs for several different files to be merged into one, using a header for each modified file that may look something like this:%0A%0A Index: path/to/file.cpp%0A%0AThe special case, with files that do not end end in a newline is not handled. Neither the unidiff utility nor the POSIX diff standard define a way to handle this type of files. %0AThe [[Patch (Unix)%7Cpatch]] program is not aware even of a implementation specific diff output.%0A%0A%3D%3D%3D Others %3D%3D%3D%0APostprocessors '''sdiff''' and '''diffmk''' render side%2Dby%2Dside diff listings and applied change marks to printed documents, respectively.  Both were developed elsewhere in Bell Labs in or before 1981.%0A%0A[[|=4|+3]]|=1|+compares one f|=1|+le again|=1|+t|=1|+two other files.  It was originally d|=3|+loped by Paul Jensen to |=1|+econcile changes made b|=2|+t|=2|+ people editing a common sou|=1|+ce.  It is also use|=2|+in|=1|+ernall|=1|+ by many revision control systems.%0A%0AGNU diff and diff3 are included in the '''diffutils''' |=1|+ackage with other diff and [[Patch (Unix)%7Cpatch]] related utilities.  [[Emacs]] has [[Ediff]] for showing the changes a patch would provide in a user interface that combines interactive editing and merging capabilities for patch files.%0A%0A[[Wdiff]] shows the words or phrases that changed in a text document of written language even in the presence of word%2Dwrapping or different column widths. Spiff compares files but ignores floating point differences with tunable precision and dismisses irrelevant code such as whitespace and comment formatting.<ref>http://hpux.connect.org.uk/hppd/hpux/Text/spiff%2D1.0/</ref>  Utilities that compare source files by their syntactic structure have been built mostly as research tools for some programming languages;<ref>Horwitz, Susan. ''Identifying the semantic and textual differences between two versions of a program''.  ACM SIGPLAN Notices 25(6). pp. 234%2D245. June 1989.</ref><ref>Yang, Wuu. ''Identifying Syntactic Differences Between Two Programs''.  Software%2D%2DPractice and Experienc|=4|+ol. 21(7), 739%2D755 (July 1991)</r|=1|+f><|=1|+ef>Grass. Cdiff: A syntax directed Diff for C%2B%2B programs. Proceedings USENIX C%2B%2B Conf., pp. 181%2D193, 1992</ref> some are availa|=1|+le as commercial tools <ref>Compare%2B%2B|=2|+http://www.coodesoft.com/</ref><ref>SmartDifferencer, http://www.semanticdesigns.com/Products/SmartDifferencer</ref><ref>Cheney, Austin. ''Pretty Diff %2D Documentation''. http://prettydiff.com/documentation.php</ref>  Tools exist to compare HTML <ref>''DaisyDiff'', http://code.google.com/p/daisydiff/</ref> and ones for XML have been published by Microsoft and IBM.<ref>''xmldiffpatch'', http://msdn.microsoft.com/en%2Dus/library/aa302294.aspx</ref><ref>''xmldiffmerge'', http://www.alphaworks.ibm.com/tech/xmldiffmerge</ref>%0A%0A%3D%3DFree file comparison tools%3D%3D%0A{{colbegin}}%0A*[[cmp (Unix)%7Ccmp]]%0A*[[comm]]%0A*[[diff3]]%0A*[[Kompare]]%0A*[[tkdiff]]%0A*[[WinMerge]] (windows)%0A*[[Unified Code Count(UCC)%7CUCC]]%0A*[[meld (software)%7Cmeld]]%0A*[http://www.diffchecker.com/ Diff Checker] (web based)%0A*[http://www.aldram.com/Diff.aspx Web Diff] (web based)%0A*[http://prettydiff.com/ Pretty Diff] (web based)%0A*[http://sourcegear.com/diffmerge DiffMerge]%0A{{colend}}%0A%0A%3D%3DSee also%3D%3D%0A{{colbegin}}%0A*[[Comparison of file comparison tools]]%0A*[[Delta encoding]]%0A*[[Difference operator]]%0A*[[Edit distance]]%0A**[[Levenshtein distance]]%0A*[[History of software configuration management]]%0A*[[Longest common subsequence problem]]%0A*[[Microsoft File Compare]]%0A*[[Revision control]]%0A*[[Software configuration management]]%0A{{colend}}%0A%0A%3D%3D|=2|+tes%3D%3D%0A{{reflist}}%0A*{{cite jo|=1|+r|=1|+al%7Cauthor%3DPaul|=1|+H|=2|+kel%7C|=1|+itle%3DA technique for isolating differences between files%7Cjournal%3DCommunications of the ACM%7Cvolume%3D21%7Cissue%3D4%7Cmonth%3DApril %7C year%3D1978%7Cpages%3D264&#x2013;268%7Cdoi%3D10|=1|+1145/359460.359467}}[http://doi.acm.org/10.1145/359460.359467]%0A*A generic implementation of the Myers SES/LCS algorithm with the Hirschberg linear space refinement [http://www.ioplex.com/~miallen/libmba/dl/src/diff.c (C source code)]%0A%0A%3D%3DExternal links%3D%3D%0A<!%2D%2D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D({{No More Links}})%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%0A    %7C PLEASE BE CAUTIOUS IN ADDING MORE LINKS TO THIS ARTICLE. WIKIPEDIA  %7C%0A    %7C IS NOT A COLLECTION OF LINKS NOR SHOULD IT BE USED FOR ADVERTISING. %7C%0A    %7C                                                                     %7C%0A    %7C           Excessive or inappropriate links WILL BE DELETED.         %7C%0A    %7C See [[Wikipedia:External links]] & [[Wikipedia:Spam]] for details.  %7C%0A    %7C                                                                     %7C%0A    %7C If there are already plentiful links, please propose additions or   %7C%0A    %7C replacements on this article's discussion page, or submit your link %7C%0A    %7C to the relevant category at the Open Directory Project (dmoz.org)   %7C%0A    %7C and link back to that category using the {{dmoz}} template.         %7C%0A    %3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D({{No More Links}})%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%3D%2D%2D>%0A*{{man%7Ccu%7Cdiff%7CSUS%7Ccompare two files}}%0A*{{Dmoz%7CComputers/Software/File_Management/File_Comparison%7CFile comparison}}%0A*[http://www.gnu.org/software/diffutils/ GNU Diff utilities]. Made available by the [[Free Software Foundation]]. Free Documentation. Free source code.%0A*[http://www.bodurov.com/JavaScriptDiff/ JavaScript Implementation]%0A%0A{{Unix commands}}%0A{{Revision control software}}%0A%0A[[Category:1974 software]]%0A[[Category:Free file comparison tools]]%0A[[Category:Formal languages]]%0A[[Category:Pattern matching]]%0A[[Category:Data differencing]]%0A[[Category:Standard Unix programs]]%0A[[Category:Unix SUS2008 utilities]]%0A%0A[[ar:Diff]]%0A[[cs:Diff]]%0A[[da:Diff]]%0A[[de:Diff]]%0A[[el:Diff]]%0A[[es:Diff]]%0A[[fr:Diff]]%0A[[ko:Diff]]%0A[[it:Diff]]%0A[[hu:Diff]]%0A[[ja:Diff]]%0A[[pl:Diff]]%0A[[pt:Diff]]%0A[[ru:Diff]]%0A[[fi:Diff]]%0A[[sv:Diff]]%0A[[tr:Diff]]%0A[[uk:Diff]]%0A[[zh:Diff]]|
